Since llvm-23, libunwind provides the canonical definition of the
`__cpp_exception` tag itself. This goes together with the removal of
WasmException::endModule() in llvm-22, which used to emit that definition
from the backend.

Arcadia builds wasm targets with an older clang, whose backend still emits it,
so the definition below collides with the backend one:

    error: symbol '__cpp_exception' is already defined

Keep the libunwind-side definition for llvm-22 and newer only.

--- contrib/libs/libunwind/src/Unwind-wasm.c
+++ contrib/libs/libunwind/src/Unwind-wasm.c
@@ -75,6 +75,12 @@ _Unwind_RaiseException(_Unwind_Exception *exception_object) {
 // definition for this API and it's required for users to ensure that there's
 // only one copy of `libunwind` within a wasm module to ensure this is only
 // defined once and exactly once.
+//
+// Up to LLVM 21 the backend emitted this definition on its own from
+// WasmException::endModule(), so with an older toolchain defining it here as
+// well makes the assembler fail with
+//   error: symbol '__cpp_exception' is already defined
+#if !defined(__clang_major__) || __clang_major__ >= 22
 __asm__(".globl __cpp_exception\n"
 #if defined(__wasm32__)
         ".tagtype __cpp_exception i32\n"
@@ -84,6 +90,7 @@ _Unwind_RaiseException(_Unwind_Exception *exception_object) {
 #error "Unsupported Wasm architecture"
 #endif
         "__cpp_exception:\n");
+#endif
 
 /// Called by __cxa_end_catch.
 _LIBUNWIND_EXPORT void
